  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "XceKCQ9c5CVJBzUXd0/hWeAlCsk=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:27:03.853Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "GMV's row lists four verticals \u2014 defence, aerospace, healthcare and transport \u2014 and records its platform stack as \"not researched\". Vertical fit is therefore the entire case for its presence in the priority-4 pool, and vertical fit alone cannot carry a lead: it says a firm serves buyers with the right problems, not that the firm has room or reason to place a new component.\n\nThe rank itself came from a mechanical rescore of a provisional v2 tail row, using size and slot occupancy only. No v3 evidence was gathered, which is what the flag means by \"DERIVED, NOT SCORED ON v3\", and it is why the priority field carries an unrun OSS-portfolio screen rather than a verdict.\n\nFor a firm working in defence and transport, the screen is also the cheapest way to learn something decision-relevant: what a systems house of this kind already standardises on tends to be visible in its published portfolio, and that is exactly the input the current record lacks.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**4 \u00b7 needs OSS screen**\n- GMV (ES)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> DERIVED, NOT SCORED ON v3 \u2014 v2 row rescored mechanically (size + slot-occupancy only). Run the OSS-portfolio screen before acting. v2 verticals: Defence, aerospace, healthcare, transport",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:27:03.853524Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:27:03.853528Z"
    }
  },
